meta {
  name: Ingest Lab Document (/documents/ingest)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/documents/ingest
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(fixtures/synthetic-lab-report.pdf) @contentType(application/pdf)
  patientId: {{ingestPatientId}}
  documentReferenceId: {{ingestDocumentReferenceId}}
  docType: lab_pdf
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.status: matches ^(Ingested|AlreadyIngested)$
  res.body.documentReferenceId: isString
  res.body.factCount: gt 0
}

docs {
  UC-9 (REQUIREMENTS.md / REQUIREMENTS.md) - "document-derived pre-visit brief", which extends REQUIREMENTS.md's UC-1
  and UC-3. Numbered UC-9, not UC-6: REQUIREMENTS.md originally numbered its own Week 2 use cases starting
  from 6, colliding with REQUIREMENTS.md's own UC-6 ("On-demand day's agenda", the Agenda/ requests below);
  both docs now use UC-9..UC-11 for the Week 2 set. Implements FR-DOC-1; its idempotency is
  FR-DOC-3.

  This is the pre-visit half of the evidence flow. The front desk uploads a document through
  OpenEMR's own Documents workflow; the `oe-module-agentforge` ingestion cron then calls this
  endpoint with the bytes plus the OpenEMR `DocumentReference` id, so by the time the clinician
  opens the patient the facts are already derived and citable (ARCHITECTURE-DOCUMENTS.md §4). This request
  stands in for that cron call.

  NO SESSION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE (W2-D17). This route carries no clinician authority - it
  derives facts and makes no user-scoped FHIR call - so it authenticates by trusted origin instead:
  in the deployed topology the reverse proxy front door does not route `/documents/ingest` at all
  and the sidecar has no public domain, so only the private network reaches it. Locally there is no
  proxy in front of the bare sidecar, which is why this request needs no `sessionCookie` while
  every `Evidence/` request does. A shared-secret header is a deferred hardening option, recorded
  as such (ARCHITECTURE-DOCUMENTS.md §15 W2-D17).

  SAFE TO RE-RUN. Ingestion is idempotent on the content hash of the bytes (W2-D3), so the first
  run returns `Ingested` and every later run returns `AlreadyIngested` without re-extracting -
  hence the `matches` assertion covering both. On the `AlreadyIngested` path the response echoes
  the DocumentReference id of the *already stored* document rather than the one just sent, so this
  asserts the field's shape rather than its value; changing `ingestDocumentReferenceId` without
  also changing the fixture's bytes will not change what comes back.

  `factCount > 0` IS THE REAL ASSERTION. A document whose extraction fails the strict schema gate
  persists nothing and returns 422, so a 200 with facts on it is the whole ingestion path - upload,
  vision extraction, schema gate, derived-fact persistence - having actually run.

  `ingestPatientId` DOES NOT HAVE TO MATCH THE SESSION, AND USUALLY WILL NOT. Facts are stored per
  patient, and `Evidence/Ask.bru` reads back whatever is on file for *the session's* patient. The
  environment's default is a synthetic id, so this request always runs standalone; to see these
  facts come back as citations from `Ask.bru`, set `ingestPatientId` to the id `Patient/Patient
  Context.bru` returns for your launched session. See the README's "Week 2 endpoints".
}
